diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 5785c8c0eeb16277ae8c1cdf51c297a03d11d4b6..d0e7f7717d211afb62e13d582187b9bcad44fc02 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -1,74 +1,106 @@
 # Bitcoin Cash Explorer
 
-Nuxt 3 explorer with CashTokens, address tools, and transaction analytics.
+Nuxt 3 explorer backed by a small Bitcoin Cash REST service. Blocks, transactions,
+mempool, CashTokens balances, holders, history, and NFT outputs use the configured
+API directly. The browser does not connect to Chaingraph or GraphQL.
 
 ## Development
 
+Use Node.js 24 or newer and pnpm 10.6.5:
+
 ```bash
 pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
-pnpm dev
+NUXT_PUBLIC_CASHTOKENS_API=http://127.0.0.1:3001 pnpm dev
 ```
 
-The default data source is `gql.chaingraph.pat.mn`, matching the configured
-Cloudflare build variables. Override it using build-time environment variables:
+Set these public build-time variables for a static deployment:
 
 ```bash
-CHAINGRAPH_HTTP=https://your-chaingraph-host/v1/graphql
-CHAINGRAPH_WS=wss://your-chaingraph-host/v1/graphql
+NUXT_PUBLIC_CASHTOKENS_API=https://tokens.example.com
+NUXT_PUBLIC_CASHTOKENS_CHIPNET_API=https://chipnet-tokens.example.com
+NUXT_PUBLIC_ELECTRUM_MAINNET_URL=wss://bch.imaginary.cash:50004
+NUXT_PUBLIC_ELECTRUM_CHIPNET_URL=wss://your-chipnet-electrum.example.com:50004
 NUXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_GATEWAY=https://ipfs.filebase.io
 ```
 
-These URLs are included in the browser bundle; do not include private credentials.
-`NUXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_GATEWAY` should be the gateway origin (without `/ipfs`); it can
-be replaced with any IPFS HTTP gateway your deployment is allowed to use. The
-legacy `IPFS_GATEWAY` variable is also supported for local builds.
-
-The Settings page includes these public gateway options:
-
-- Filebase: `https://ipfs.filebase.io`
-- Pinata: `https://gateway.pinata.cloud`
-- 4EVERLAND: `https://4everland.io`
-- Web3.Storage: `https://w3s.link`
-
-The selected gateway is stored in the browser and overrides the deployment
-default for that browser.
+`NUXT_PUBLIC_CASHTOKENS_API` is the mainnet API origin, without `/v1`; the same
+service supplies BCH and CashTokens routes. The chipnet API is separate. The
+Settings dialog selects the network. An absent or mismatched provider produces
+an explicit error; chipnet never falls back to a mainnet provider. No private RPC
+credentials belong in these variables or the browser bundle. Add the explorer's
+origin to the API's CORS allowlist.
+
+Addresses use network-specific Electrum WebSocket providers for BCH balances and
+address transaction history; full transactions then come from the REST API. The
+client checks the provider's genesis hash and a Bitcoin Cash checkpoint
+(mainnet's CashTokens activation block or chipnet's fork block), rejecting Bitcoin
+Core, BSV, and testnet4 providers. Chipnet requires its own configured provider.
+The mainnet Electrum URL above is the deployment default. HTTPS explorers require
+HTTPS REST and WSS Electrum URLs.
+
+## REST contract
+
+Responses carry `{ network, confirmed_only, data }`. Token amounts and satoshi
+values are decimal strings, and byte arrays use PostgreSQL-style `\\x` hex
+prefixes. The UI keeps large token amounts exact using BigNumber.
+
+- `GET /v1/bch/tip`
+- `GET /v1/bch/blocks?limit=7&offset=0`
+- `GET /v1/bch/blocks/:heightOrHash?limit=9&offset=0`
+- `GET /v1/bch/transactions/:txid`
+- `GET /v1/bch/transactions?ids=txid1,txid2`
+- `GET /v1/bch/mempool?limit=9&offset=0`
+- `GET /v1/tokens/:category`
+- `GET /v1/tokens/:category/holders?limit=9&offset=0`
+- `GET /v1/tokens/:category/transactions?limit=9&offset=0`
+- `GET /v1/tokens/:category/utxos?nft=true&limit=9&offset=0`
+- `GET /v1/scripts/:lockingBytecode/tokens?limit=9&offset=0`
+- `GET /v1/scripts/:lockingBytecode/utxos?nft=true&limit=9&offset=0`
+
+Token views show confirmed supply in base units, NFT output counts, minting
+capabilities, aggregated holder balances, and history including complete burns.
+Mempool transactions remain unconfirmed. Unknown BCH input values or block totals
+remain unavailable rather than being reported as zero. BCH transaction lookups
+require the service's BCHN node to have transaction indexing enabled.
+
+## Metadata
+
+Public BCMR registries still provide names, icons, and descriptions. Their origin
+is shown on token pages. The explorer does not infer ownership, reserve supply,
+or verified authchains from a registry entry. The Doctor page validates a supplied
+registry URL or BCMR OP_RETURN, schema, and an optional expected content hash; a
+matching user-supplied hash does not establish on-chain ownership or freshness.
+
+The default public metadata registry is
+`https://otr.cash/.well-known/bitcoin-cash-metadata-registry.json`. The default
+IPFS gateway is `https://ipfs.filebase.io`; Settings can override it per browser.
 
 ## Verification and deployment
 
 ```bash
+pnpm test
 pnpm lint:type
 pnpm generate
 wrangler deploy --dry-run
 wrangler deploy
 ```
 
-Wrangler runs a fresh static build and uploads `.output/public` to the `explorer`
-Worker, with SPA routing for address, transaction, block, and token URLs.
-The configured custom domain is `explorer.salemkode.com`.
-
-Use Wrangler 4 and authenticate with `wrangler login` before deploying.
-The Cloudflare account must manage the `salemkode.com` zone. An existing DNS record
-for the custom domain may need replacement when moving from another host.
-
-## Automatic GitHub deployments
-
-Cloudflare Workers Builds is connected to `salemkode/explorer`:
+Wrangler rebuilds and uploads `.output/public` with SPA routing. The configured
+custom domain is `explorer.salemkode.com`. Use Wrangler 4 and the Cloudflare account
+managing `salemkode.com`. pnpm's lockfile is authoritative; obsolete npm and Bun
+lockfiles were removed so builds resolve the same dependencies.
 
-- Pushes to `main` build and publish to `https://explorer.salemkode.com`.
-- Pushes to other branches build and upload a version without replacing production.
-- The build command is `bun run build`; production uses `npx wrangler deploy`.
-- Non-production branches use `npx wrangler versions upload`.
-- Set `CHAINGRAPH_HTTP`, `CHAINGRAPH_WS`, and `NUXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_GATEWAY` under
-  Cloudflare's build variables.
+For Cloudflare Workers Builds, set the install command to
+`pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, the build command to `pnpm generate`, and the
+public variables above under build variables. Production uses `npx wrangler deploy`;
+non-production branches can use `npx wrangler versions upload`. Remove the old
+`CHAINGRAPH_HTTP` and `CHAINGRAPH_WS` variables, which are no longer read.
 
-Push changes to a task branch, check its Cloudflare build, and merge the pull
-request manually in GitHub when ready to publish. Do not commit or push directly
-to the protected `main` branch.
+Push only task branches. The user must merge pull requests manually; do not push
+to protected `main` or `master`.
 
 ## Traffic analytics
 
-Free Cloudflare Web Analytics is installed through the script in `nuxt.config.mts`.
-View visits, page views, referrers, and performance in the Cloudflare account's
-**Analytics → Web Analytics → explorer.salemkode.com** dashboard. The beacon token
-is a public site identifier, not an API credential. Future builds retain the
-tracking script automatically.
+Cloudflare Web Analytics remains configured in `nuxt.config.mts`. The beacon token
+is a public site identifier. Visit the Cloudflare Web Analytics dashboard to see
+visits, page views, referrers, and performance.
